33
00:01:28,598 --> 00:01:32,161
Chapter two. The coin box.

34
00:01:32,144 --> 00:01:34,191
Ralph Baer was an engineer with a

35
00:01:34,211 --> 00:01:36,698
question: if almost every home had a

36
00:01:36,718 --> 00:01:38,869
television... why was it only used for

37
00:01:38,889 --> 00:01:39,840
watching?

38
00:01:41,078 --> 00:01:43,450
By nineteen sixty-eight he had a

39
00:01:43,470 --> 00:01:46,375
prototype. In nineteen seventy-two it

40
00:01:46,395 --> 00:01:49,557
went on sale as the Magnavox Odyssey: the

41
00:01:49,577 --> 00:01:51,468
first home console.

42
00:01:51,598 --> 00:01:54,306
One of its games was ping-pong. A young

43
00:01:54,326 --> 00:01:56,977
man named Nolan Bushnell tried it at a

44
00:01:56,997 --> 00:01:59,751
demonstration. Weeks later, he founded

45
00:01:59,771 --> 00:02:00,827
Atari.

46
00:02:00,986 --> 00:02:04,112
His engineer, Al Alcorn, built his own

47
00:02:04,132 --> 00:02:06,910
version inside a wooden cabinet. They

48
00:02:06,930 --> 00:02:09,465
left it in a California bar, just to see

49
00:02:09,485 --> 00:02:10,958
